  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nToday - The Weekend: Warm Temperatures Continue, Rain Chances \nReturn Later in the Week\n\nAfter a couple of hot and humid days, the ridge that has been \nsitting over the CONUS for awhile now, shifts ever so slightly \nwestward. This will keep the area in northwest flow aloft. A \nsurface high also moves over the region. Both of these will help\nkeep those upper 70s and mid 80s dewpoints away and keep the \narea mostly dry through Wednesday. There have been some models \nthat try to bring some shower or storm activity into west-\ncentral Wisconsin and eastern Minnesota late Wednesday afternoon\nas the surface high shifts eastward allowing for enough \ninstability, 1000 to 2000 J/kg of MUCAPE from the 00Z HRRR, to \nbuild in. There are some ensemble members that support \nmeasurable precipitation across these areas on Wednesday, \nhowever not much looks to expected for rain amounts. \n\nHeading into Thursday/Friday a more defined shortwave trough \nthat moves into the Upper Midwest. This trough becomes closed \noff as it shifts more over our CWA by Friday morning. At the \nsame time, the low-level jet increases overnight Thursday into \nFriday. Moisture transport is also good as this jet will have a \nfetch all the way from the Gulf allowing for PWATs to be in the \n1.75 to 2.0\" range. This low then moves off to the east by \nFriday evening. While there are still timing differences, this \nhas the potential for heavy rain to occur. There are a few GEFS \nand EPS members that have 3+ inches of rain over parts of the \nCWA. WPC has went ahead and put the majority of our forecast \narea in a slight risk for excessive rainfall as a result of the \nparameters in place. All this being said, this will be something\nto watch as we get closer to Thursday. \n\nTemperatures over the next couple of days remain in the mid \n80s, however the dewpoints will be a bit lower than what we have\nexperienced over the last few days, mainly in the 60s. \nDepending on the track of the low on Friday, temperatures could \nbe on the cooler side if it rains for much of the day. Once this\nlow is out of the Upper Midwest, temperatures look to rebound \nback into the mid 80s.",
